SAND is a dynamically-typed, object-oriented scripting language.
SAND code is organized into classes, and these classes may be spread across multiple .sand files.
A SAND program starts executing at the top level of the entry file (such as main.sand) and runs its statements sequentially.
In SAND, a single-line comment begins with // and continues to the end of the line.
SAND has no multi-line comment syntax; only // single-line comments exist.
In SAND, a variable is created simply by assigning to it with =, and no declaration keyword is needed.
In SAND, the type of a variable is inferred at runtime rather than declared.
SAND supports integer values, for example 42.
SAND supports float values, for example 3.14.
SAND supports string values written in double quotes, for example "hello".
SAND supports array values written in square brackets, for example [1, "two", 3].
SAND supports object values, which are produced either by return_all or by instantiating a class.
SAND supports lambda values, which are anonymous functions.
SAND has no null, None, or nil value.
In SAND, the value -1 is conventionally used as a sentinel to represent "not found" or failure, because the language has no null/None/nil.
SAND's arithmetic operators are + (add), - (subtract), * (multiply), and / (divide).
SAND's comparison operators are ==, !=, <, >, <=, and >=.
In SAND, using + on two strings concatenates them, so "hello" + " world" produces "hello world".
SAND never implicitly converts between numbers and strings, so combining a number and a string with + requires explicit conversion using str() or int().
In SAND, a while loop is written as: while (condition) { body }
In SAND, an if statement is written as: if (condition) { body }
In SAND, an if/else statement is written as: if (condition) { body } else { body }
SAND has no for loop.
SAND has no break statement for exiting a loop early.
SAND has no continue statement for skipping to the next loop iteration.
SAND has no "else if" or "elif" statement.
In SAND, all loops and branches must be written using only while and if/else.
In SAND, iterating over a sequence must be done with a while loop and a manually managed index variable.
In SAND, an array literal is written with square brackets, for example arr = [1, 2, 3].
In SAND, an array element is read with arr[i], where i is the index.
In SAND, an array element is assigned with arr[i] = value.
In SAND, an element of a nested array is accessed with arr[i][j].
In SAND, an array can hold elements of mixed types in the same array.
In SAND, arr.length() returns the number of elements in the array arr.
In SAND, arr.append(val) adds val to the end of the array arr and returns nothing.
In SAND, arr.pop(i) removes the element at index i from the array arr and returns nothing.
SAND arrays have no .remove() method, so an element cannot be removed by value; it can only be removed by index with .pop(i).
The only array methods in SAND are .length(), .append(val), and .pop(i).
SAND has no array or record literal with named fields.
In SAND, a structured record is built either as a fixed-index array (for example [id, name, price, stock]) or as an object returned by return_all.
In SAND, a function must always be defined inside a class; functions cannot be defined outside a class.
In SAND, a function is defined with the def keyword, as in: def functionName(param1, param2) { body }
SAND has no explicit return keyword for normal function returns.
In SAND, the value of the last expression evaluated in a function body is automatically returned as the function's result.
In SAND, when return_all is the last statement of a function, it collects all local variables currently in scope and packs them into a single returned object.
In SAND, the caller reads each value in an object returned by return_all with dot notation using the variable's name; for example, if a function sets count = 10 and then uses return_all, the caller can read result.count to get 10.
In SAND, return_all is the standard way for a function to return multiple named values.
In SAND, the return_all keyword may only appear as the very last statement of a function body.
In SAND, an anonymous function is created with the lambda keyword, as in: lambda(param1, param2) { body }
In SAND, a lambda is a closure: it captures the variables of the scope in which it was created.
In SAND, a lambda can be assigned to a variable.
In SAND, a lambda can be returned from a method.
In SAND, a lambda stored in a variable is called with ordinary call syntax, for example myLambda("hello").
In SAND, a lambda keeps access to its captured variables even when it is called later, after the function that created it has finished; for example, a method makePrinter(prefix) can return lambda(msg) { print(prefix + msg) }, and calling the returned lambda still uses prefix.
In SAND, a class is declared with the class keyword, as in: class ClassName { fields and methods }
In SAND, a class field is declared in the class body by simple assignment, for example field1 = defaultValue.
In SAND, a class method is declared in the class body with the def keyword.
In SAND, the value assigned to a field in the class body is its default value, and it serves as a template for each instance.
In SAND, inside any method, the keyword this refers to the current instance of the class.
In SAND, an instance field must be accessed inside a method by writing this explicitly, for example this.field1.
In SAND, another method of the same instance must be called inside a method by writing this explicitly, for example this.method1(x).
In SAND, writing a field name inside a method without this does not touch the instance field; it reads or creates a local variable instead.
In SAND, a class is instantiated with ClassName.new, for example obj = ClassName.new.
In SAND, .new takes no arguments and is written without parentheses, so ClassName.new() and ClassName.new(x) are both invalid.
SAND has no constructor that takes arguments; if an object needs initialization, a separate setup method must be called after creating it with ClassName.new.
In SAND, a class inherits from another class using extends, as in: class Child extends Parent { ... }
In SAND, a child class inherits all fields and all methods of its parent class.
In SAND, a child class accesses inherited parent fields and calls inherited parent methods through this, for example this.parentMethod().
SAND has no super keyword.
In SAND, a class from another user-written .sand file is imported with: from filename import ClassName
In SAND, the filename in "from filename import ClassName" is the .sand file's name without the .sand extension.
In SAND, after "from filename import ClassName", ClassName can be used in the current file.
In SAND, a built-in standard library module is imported with: import ModuleName
In SAND, after "import ModuleName", the module's functions are called through ModuleName as a namespace, for example ModuleName.function().
In SAND, "from ... import ..." is used only for user-defined .sand files, and "import ..." is used only for built-in standard library modules.
In SAND, import and from ... import statements may only appear at the global (top) level of a file and cannot be used inside a function or inside a class.
In SAND, the built-in functions print, input, str, and int are available globally without any import.
In SAND, print(value) prints value to standard output followed by a newline.
In SAND, input() reads one line from standard input and returns it as a string.
In SAND, str(x) converts a value x of any type into its string representation.
In SAND, int(s) converts a string s into an integer.